//
// GENERATED — by scratch/atlas/bake-land.js (Natural Earth -> 0.1° runs) and
// then scratch/atlas/rechunk-land.js (one resolution, by extraction). Do not
// hand-edit: re-run the chain instead, or the next run silently reverts you.
//
// Natural Earth physical "land", public domain (CC0) — provenance and licence
// in SOURCES.md, beside every other crate this page ships. One source file:
//   1:50m  ne_50m_land.geojson  sha256 e874b27a51d14645…  → RUNS
//
// ONE CHUNKY RESOLUTION, 2026-08-27 — Paul: "Remove the high res map and keep
// the globe one chunky resolution too." Until this round the file shipped
// THREE tiers: a 0.8° COARSE table drawn while moving above 60° of arc, this
// 0.1° world table, and a 207.5 KB PATCH table — 1:10m coastline in ±2° boxes
// around the 62 PLACES, drawn under 6° of arc. The patch tier is the "high res
// map" and it is REMOVED, not refined: a chunky low-poly coastline at city
// zoom is the design now (the hard-edged enamel read), not a defect the next
// bake should fix. WHAT NEARLY LEFT WITH THE PATCHES, AND THE 2026-08-27
// REPAIR: the bake's island floor was 3 deg² (MINA), so Jamaica (~0.9 deg²),
// Trinidad (~0.4) and Puerto Rico (~0.7) were never in this 0.1° world table
// at all — their coastline came ONLY from the deleted patch tier, and
// Kingston, a marquee anchor with records on it, stood on open sea at every
// zoom. An earlier draft of this header shipped that as intended ("the sea is
// what one resolution honestly holds there") and the sentence is REVERSED
// here, not deleted: a PLACES dot is a promise that a record comes from
// somewhere, and somewhere is land — Paul never asked for Jamaica by name
// because a place standing on its own island goes without saying. MINA is
// now 0.2 deg²: every PLACE-hosting ring measures >= 0.40 deg² at the 0.1°
// tolerance (Jamaica 0.92, Puerto Rico 0.66, Long Island 0.42, Trinidad
// 0.40), so the smallest anchor island carries 2x headroom, and a PKEEP
// place-box keep in the bake means no future dot can be orphaned by the
// constant again. Measured cost: 56 -> 239 rings, 8,219 -> 9,881 points —
// see MINA and PKEEP in bake-land.js for the whole argument. The
// coarse tier is removed with the patches, on a measurement rather
// than on taste: the moving frame it existed to protect draws the full 0.1°
// rings at p50 1.8 ms / p95 4.8 ms at 390x844 (ui/globe.js tiers() carries the
// method), which is under half the budget — re-measured 2026-08-27 WITH the
// 239-ring island table, same scripted 3 s drag at 180° of arc: p50 2.02 ms /
// p95 4.92 ms, still a quarter of the 8 ms line. One table, every zoom,
// moving or settled.
//
// THE TOLERANCE ARGUMENT, REVERSED WHERE IT POINTED TIGHTER. It used to read
// "the tolerance is set by the tightest view, not the widest", and that is how
// 0.1° was chosen: the first bake's 1.0° rendered Britain as a five-sided blob
// with no Wales and no Cornwall at ELEVEN degrees of longitude, so the world
// view is still owed 0.1°. But the sentence then argued PAST 0.1° — "0.1° of
// error is 78 CSS px at 0.5° of arc, so ±2° city boxes are re-baked from 1:10m
// at 0.01° on top of it" — and that continuation is the reversed part: at the
// tightest view the 78 px facets are now what the picture is SUPPOSED to look
// like. The tolerance is set by the tightest view THAT IS MEANT TO LOOK
// SMOOTH, which is the ~11° regional read, and that view keeps 0.1°.
//
// ANTARCTICA IS BACK. The first two bakes dropped it — "it is entirely below
// VIEWS["the world"].lat0 (-58)" — which was true of a plate carrée behind a
// table of rectangles and is false of a globe you can turn to the south pole.
// See ICE in the bake script: the reason is reversed there, not deleted.
//
// SHAPE — two named tables, one resolution, and each has one job:
//
